#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125825 :tutorial/content "What is the directional derivative of the function $\\mathbf{f}$ at $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of the gradient? \n\nLet's apply our formula. First, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\nabla\\! f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}){‖}^{2}}{‖\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =‖\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude the following:\n\n*The directional derivative in the direction of the gradient equals the norm of the gradient. Furthermore, the norm of the gradient can be interpreted as the rate of change of the function in the direction of the gradient.*" :tutorial/title "The Rate of Change in the Direction of the Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125835 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}t)(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\  & =-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125836 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) &=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) &=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Vector Form of the Multivariable Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125837 :tutorial/content "Given a function $f(x,y),$ how do we calculate the partial derivatives $f_r$ and $f_\\theta,$ where $(r,\\theta)$ are plane polar coordinates?\n\nTo do this, we can use the multivariable chain rule. First, let's write down our tree diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f19c4a682b30f1eea1d9c1be7da83bd77ba137173f25eb9c7e5cf253abba015.png)\n\nFrom the tree diagram, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take the partial derivatives of $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the multivariable chain rule, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125838 :tutorial/content "Given a function of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ we can find formulas for $f_r, f_\\theta,$ and $f_z,$ where $(r,\\theta,z)$ are cylindrical polar coordinates. The results that we need are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first and second equations are identical to the equations derived for plane polar coordinates, as we would expect. \n\nThe difference here is that we now have a third equation that deals with the $z$-dependence. But since the $z$-coordinate is the same in both Cartesian and cylindrical polar coordinates, we can simply differentiate with respect to $z$ as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125847 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've been using the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{r} & ={f}_{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {f}_{\\theta } & =-r{f}_{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can treat the above as a system of equations in the \"unknowns\" $f_x$ and $f_y.$ Solving this system gives explicit expressions for $f_x$ and $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$\n\nFor example, suppose we want an explicit formula for $f_x.$ We can eliminate $f_y$ by multiplying the first equation by $r\\cos\\theta$ and the second equation by $\\sin\\theta$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r{f}_{r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =r{f}_{x}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {f}_{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-r{f}_{x}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\nr f_r \\cos\\theta - f_\\theta \\sin\\theta = r f_x(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nf_x = f_r \\cos\\theta - \\dfrac1r f_\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can use a similar procedure to write $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$ Our results as summarized below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\cos \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin \\theta\\\\[1ex]\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\sin \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\cos \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Differentiating of a Function Given in Polar Coordinates With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125848 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a function $f(x,y,z),$ and we wish to find formulas for $f_\\rho,$ $f_\\theta,$ and $f_\\phi$, where $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ are spherical polar coordinates.\n\nUsing the multivariable chain rule, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\rho } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂ϕ} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might want to try deriving these formulas for yourself!" :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125865 :tutorial/content "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system. \n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$\n(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." :tutorial/title "The Graph of a Two-Variable Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125868 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125869 :tutorial/content "The **domain** of a multivariable function is the set of points where the function is defined. \n\nFor example, consider the function \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}.\n$$\n\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the expression under the square root is non-negative. As a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 - x^2 - y^2 \\geq 0 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + y^2 \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the domain of our function is a disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane, as shown on the left diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/8642db9fd933ec2f15c6712c713cfaec8fb20052066dcca3915904190d3551fa.png)\n\nEach point in this domain is uniquely mapped to a real number $z.$ Possible values of $z$ can be visualized on a separate axis (depicted by the diagram on the right). \n\nIf $(x,y)$ is allowed to run over the entire domain, the set of points on the $z$-axis where the values of $f(x,y)$ land is called the **range** of the function. For this function, the range is $z\\in [0,1].$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When discussing a function's domain, we usually refer to the so-called **natural domain**, the largest set of values for which the function is defined.\n\n- We can also *restrict* the domain of a function to a subset of the natural domain. For example, we might restrict the domain of $f$ to all values of $y$ in the upper-half plane. In this case, we'd write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}, \\quad (x,y)\\in D, \\quad D = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: y\\geq 0 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis gives a domain/range diagram that looks as follows.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab832d984232b0b3b951579e5ee2727e92c9f4bf496d4fffe82f7230cfbb14.png)\n\nIn this case, restricting the domain does not affect the range. However, in general, restricting the domain *will* affect the range." :tutorial/title "The Domain and Range of a Two-Variable Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130392 :tutorial/content "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions. \n\nLet  $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \n\nRecall that, under this notation, \n\n- $\\mathbf u = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]}$ represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n\n- $\\mathbf x = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}$ represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} = \\mathbf{T}\\left( {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\right) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\cos   v \\\\ u \\sin   v\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that \n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130404 :tutorial/content "Evaluating double integrals by changing the variables to polar coordinates is often easier than using Cartesian coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the disk with center $(0,0)$ and radius $2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/94/945f3881b5348fffb922d7b57fb42a6262eb03861fb5aa2411b947a16dcb3b59.png)\n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to plane polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $D$ is a disc of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ which has a simple polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ can also be expressed simply in polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined by\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9901b20d218bef2935dba64612495874d08388dc6d7ae21f8a1de596296bc276.png)\n\n             \n\nTo express our integral in terms of polar coordinates, we will use the **change of variables formula,** given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, {{\\color{black}{\\mathrm d A}}} = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  {{\\color{black}{r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta}}}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{\\textrm d A}} = {\\color{black}{r\\,\\textrm d r\\textrm d \\theta}}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of $r.$\n\nNow, applying the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{black}\\mathrm{d}A} & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{black}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = 8\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn terms of transformations, the change of variables formula maps the non-rectangular integration domain $D$ to the rectangular domain $\\Delta,$ making the integral much easier to evaluate." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
